  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Rebuilt on TestFramework.Container 0.1.1, which is where the change is.\nChanged in 0.1.1:\n- Building an image from a project no longer depends on the registry being reachable, and no longer rebuilds the same image for every environment. A suite of a dozen chapters against one unchanged application used to pay for a dozen identical publishes; it now pays for one. Measured on a web suite: 25 minutes down to under 3.\n- An image the Docker daemon cannot pull is fetched by the test process over IPv4 and loaded in, so a broken IPv6 path to a registry no longer stops a run on a machine nobody is allowed to reconfigure.\n- Every dotnet invocation is bounded and killed with its process tree, so a wedged publish cannot hang a run or outlive it.\nPreviously: Initial release: container-backed SQL Server, application and stubbed dependencies for web timelines.\nWorth knowing up front:\n- A container reused across runs keeps whatever the previous run wrote, so SqlResetMode decides what happens to the database between runs - recreate it, run a reset script, or leave it alone.\n- Published ports bind to 127.0.0.1 rather than every interface, and the SQL Server password is generated per environment instead of being a constant shipped in the package. Use UseSqlPassword(...) to pin it, which a reused container needs.\n- Where the application under test comes from is declared rather than guessed, through the same ContainerSource model as TestFramework.Container: an image, a project path, or a directory. The resolved plan is logged before anything is built.",
